#align(center)[#text(size: 16pt, weight: "bold")[Thompson's group $F$ is nonamenable]]
#align(center)[OpenAI]
#align(center)[September 23, 2026]

== 1 Introduction <sec:introduction>

Thompson’s group $F$ consists of the increasing piecewise linear homeomorphisms of $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$ with finitely many pieces, dyadic rational breakpoints, and slopes in $2^(bb(Z))$. We use the multiplication convention $h g = h compose g$ and regard $F$ as a discrete group.

A discrete group $G$ is #emph[amenable] if its bounded real-valued functions admit a positive normalized left-invariant mean: a linear functional $M : ell^oo \( G \; bb(R) \) arrow.r bb(R)$ satisfying $M \( 1 \) = 1$, $M \( phi \) gt.eq 0$ whenever $phi gt.eq 0$, and $M \( g mapsto phi \( h g \) \) = M \( phi \)$ for every $h in G$. This invariant-mean viewpoint belongs to the classical theory developed by von Neumann and Day #cite(label("vonNeumann1929")) #cite(label("Day1957")). We use the following forward implication of the Følner criterion #cite(label("Folner1955")): if $G$ is amenable, then for every finite $S subset G$ and every $epsilon > 0$, there is a nonempty finite $A subset G$ such that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.1)")
 $ frac(\| h A triangle.stroked.t A \|, \| A \|) < epsilon #h(2em) \( h in S \) . $ <eq:folner>]

The modern left-translation formulation in #cite(label("Moore2013")) Theorem 1.2 bounds the sum of these ratios and hence implies each strict individual bound by taking a smaller tolerance. Thus amenability requires finite sets whose relative boundaries are simultaneously small under any fixed finite family of translations.

Thompson introduced $F$ in 1965; an early published construction appears in McKenzie and Thompson #cite(label("McKenzieThompson1973")), with the identification explained by Cannon, Floyd, and Parry #cite(label("CannonFloydParry1996")) pp. 215–216. The latter authors record Geoghegan’s 1979 conjecture that $F$ is nonamenable #cite(label("CannonFloydParry1996")) p. 227. We prove this conjecture.

#thm([Theorem 1.1.])[
  #emph[Thompson’s group $F$ is not amenable.]
] <thm:main>

===== 1.1 Context and prior work. <context-and-prior-work.>

Two classical structural facts help explain the difficulty of the amenability problem. Brin and Squier show that $F$ contains no nonabelian free subgroup #cite(label("BrinSquier1985")) Theorem 3.1, so the familiar free-subgroup obstruction does not apply. Yet $F$ is not elementary amenable #cite(label("CannonFloydParry1996")) Theorem 4.10: it lies outside the class generated by finite and abelian groups under subgroups, quotients, extensions, and directed unions. Neither fact decides ordinary amenability. The group also has strong topological finiteness properties: Brown and Geoghegan construct a classifying space with finitely many cells in each dimension #cite(label("BrownGeoghegan1984")).

Finite approximations have provided several ways to study the remaining question. Moore proves tower lower bounds on the sizes of possible Følner sets #cite(label("MooreFolner2013")) Theorem 1.1 and characterizes amenability of $F$ by a scalar convex Ramsey property for finite rooted ordered binary trees #cite(label("Moore2013")) Theorem 3.1. Guba improves densities of finite Cayley subgraphs #cite(label("Guba2025Density")) and derives restrictions on right-invariant means from a partition into seven diagram-defined classes #cite(label("Guba2026")). From an operator-algebraic direction, Haagerup and Olesen prove that simplicity of the reduced group $C^(*)$-algebra of Thompson’s group $T$ would imply nonamenability of $F$ #cite(label("HaagerupOlesen2017")) Theorem 5.5. These results expose constraints on amenability through finite sets, trees, diagrams, and representations. The proof below uses finite dyadic partitions and scalar correlations of Hilbert-valued colors.

There have also been claims of a complete resolution in both directions. Akhmedov’s 2021 version claims nonamenability through a height-function criterion #cite(label("Akhmedov2021")). Shavgulidze claimed amenability #cite(label("Shavgulidze2009")); Moore identified errors in that approach #cite(label("Moore2011Critique")). Moore separately withdrew an amenability claim after Akhmedov identified an error in Lemma 4.13 of that manuscript #cite(label("Moore2012Withdrawn")). That withdrawal concerns a different work from his published Ramsey characterization cited above.

===== 1.2 Proof strategy. <proof-strategy.>

The proof turns approximate translation invariance of finite averages of scalar functions on $F$ into an approximate fixed point of a Lipschitz map on a Hilbert ball. The following infinite-dimensional phenomenon supplies a map for which that conclusion is impossible.

#thm([Lemma 1.2.])[
  (Benyamini–Sternfeld).  #emph[There are a real Hilbert space $H$, a Lipschitz map $f : B arrow.r B$ on its closed unit ball, and a constant $delta > 0$ such that 
$ lr(bar.v.double f \( x \) - x bar.v.double) gt.eq delta #h(2em) \( x in B \) . $

]
] <lem:displacement>

Benyamini and Sternfeld prove this assertion for every infinite-dimensional normed space #cite(label("BenyaminiSternfeld1983")) Theorem, part (3), p. 439. For completeness, Appendix 4 constructs such a map on $L^2 \( \[ 0 \, 1 \] \; bb(R)^2 \)$ with $delta = 1 \/ 2$, including all the needed Lipschitz estimates.

Fix this map $f$, a positive Lipschitz constant $L$, and a large integer $D$. A basic dyadic interval is a cell of a uniform dyadic partition, and a basic partition is a finite partition into such cells. Choose $D$ basic intervals with positive gaps between them and endpoints in $\( 0 \, 1 \)$, called parents. Put an affine copy of this family inside each parent, calling the copies descendants. When a parent is a union of partition cells, restricting to it and stretching back to $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$ gives its normalized restriction. Color each basic partition recursively by applying $f$ to the mean of its parent-restriction colors when all these restrictions are defined, and by zero otherwise. Proper restriction decreases the number of cells, so the recursion is well founded. For a sufficiently fine image partition under $g in F$, its parent colors $X_i$ and descendant colors $Y_(i j)$ therefore satisfy

$ m = 1 / D sum_(i = 1)^D X_i \, #h(2em) z_i = 1 / D sum_(j = 1)^D Y_(i j) \, #h(2em) X_i = f \( z_i \) quad \( 1 lt.eq i lt.eq D \) . $

The group $F$ can carry any ordered pair of separated basic intervals with endpoints in $\( 0 \, 1 \)$ affinely onto any other such pair. Choose a finite set $S$ of these transports comparing every separated pair among the parents and descendants with one reference pair. The set $S$ is fixed before the arbitrary nonempty finite set $A subset F$; only the common partition level, chosen fine enough for $A union S A$, may depend on $A$. Write $eta$ for the largest relative $S$-boundary of $A$. Exact covariance of normalized restrictions and finite-average cancellation make all averaged scalar correlations of separated interval colors differ from one common value by at most $eta$.

Expand the average over $A$ of $lr(bar.v.double z_i - m bar.v.double)^2$. The common correlation cancels; diagonal and nested pairs occupy only a fraction $1 \/ D$ of each relevant sum, as Figure 1 illustrates. Unit-ball bounds control those exceptions, giving a universal constant times $D^(- 1) + eta$. Since $m$ is also the mean of the $f \( z_i \)$, convexity and Lipschitz continuity bound the average squared displacement $lr(bar.v.double m - f \( m \) bar.v.double)^2$ by a constant times $L^2 \( D^(- 1) + eta \)$. For large $D$ and small $eta$, this contradicts the uniform lower bound $delta^2$. Section 2 makes the argument precise and proves a uniform positive boundary bound in Proposition 5, contradicting (1.1).

Section 3 records consequences for uniformly bounded representations and percolation, using separate companion theorems.

== 2 Recursive colors and finite averages <sec:proof>

We prove Theorem 1 using the map supplied by Lemma 2. The argument uses a single finite average over the group. We first record the dyadic facts that make its correlations comparable.

=== 2.1 Dyadic partitions and affine transport <sec:dyadic>

We use the standard dyadic-partition model of $F$; see #cite(label("CannonFloydParry1996")) Section 2, Lemma 2.2. We record the restriction and pair-transport facts needed for the finite averages.

A #emph[basic dyadic interval] is an interval

$ \[ k 2^(- r) \, \( k + 1 \) 2^(- r) \] subset.eq \[ 0 \, 1 \] \, #h(2em) r in bb(N) union { 0 } \, quad 0 lt.eq k < 2^r . $

A #emph[basic partition] is a finite partition of $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$ into such intervals; cells may share endpoints. Its mesh is the greatest length of a cell. A partition $T$ #emph[respects] an interval $I$ if $I$ is a union of cells of $T$.

For a basic interval $I$, let $s_I : \[ 0 \, 1 \] arrow.r I$ be the increasing affine map, and write $I dot.op J = s_I \( J \)$. If $T$ respects $I$, define its normalized restriction by

$ T_I = { s_I^(- 1) \( K \) : K in T \, med K subset.eq I } . $

Both $I dot.op J$ and the cells of $T_I$ are basic dyadic intervals. Indeed, if $I$ has length $2^(- r)$, a basic cell contained in $I$ has length $2^(- s)$ with $s gt.eq r$; applying $s_I^(- 1)$ gives a cell of length $2^(- \( s - r \))$ whose left endpoint is a multiple of that length. The affine charts satisfy $s_(I dot.op J) = s_I compose s_J$. Consequently, whenever $T$ respects both $I$ and $I dot.op J$, the partition $T_I$ respects $J$ and

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.1)")
 $ \( T_I \)_J = T_(I dot.op J) . $ <dyad:restriction>]

Let $T^(\( n \))$ be the uniform partition of $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$ into intervals of length $2^(- n)$. For $g in F$, the notation $g T^(\( n \))$ denotes the partition formed by the images of these cells.

#thm([Lemma 2.1.])[
  #emph[For each $g in F$, the partitions $g T^(\( n \))$ are basic for all sufficiently large $n$, and their meshes tend to zero. In particular, given a finite set $K subset F$ and a finite family $cal(J)$ of basic intervals, there is an $n_0$ such that $g T^(\( n \))$ is basic and respects every member of $cal(J)$ whenever $g in K$ and $n gt.eq n_0$.]
] <dyad:eventual>

#proof[
  The image under $g$ of each breakpoint is dyadic: starting from $g \( 0 \) = 0$, sum the increments on the preceding linear pieces, each a power of two times a dyadic length. Thus every linear piece has the form $x mapsto 2^q x + b$ with $q in bb(Z)$ and $b$ dyadic. Choose $n$ large enough to resolve all breakpoints, to have $n gt.eq q$ on every piece, and to make each corresponding $b$ an integer multiple of $2^(q - n)$. The image of each uniform cell is then a basic interval of length $2^(q - n)$. There are finitely many slopes, so the mesh tends to zero.
  Two basic intervals with intersecting interiors are nested. Hence a basic partition of mesh smaller than the length of a basic interval $I$ respects $I$: every cell whose interior meets that of $I$ must be contained in $I$. Apply this observation to the finitely many intervals in $cal(J)$, and then take the maximum of the finitely many thresholds for $g in K$. ◻
]

Write $I < J$ when $max I < min J$; in particular this notation requires a positive gap. An interval is #emph[internal] if both its endpoints lie in $\( 0 \, 1 \)$.

#thm([Lemma 2.2.])[
  #emph[If $I < J$ and $I' < J'$ are pairs of internal basic intervals, there is an $h in F$ whose restrictions carry $I$ affinely onto $I'$ and $J$ affinely onto $J'$.]
] <dyad:transport>

#proof[
  The two selected intervals leave three complementary gaps, all of positive length and with dyadic endpoints. Partition each gap into basic cells, for example by a sufficiently fine uniform dyadic grid. Do this for both pairs. Within each corresponding pair of gaps, equalize the numbers of cells by successively bisecting cells on the side with fewer cells. Each bisection increases the number by one. The source and target partitions now have the same number of cells, with the two selected intervals in matching positions. Map corresponding cells increasingly and affinely. All breakpoints are dyadic and all slopes are ratios of basic lengths, hence powers of two. This defines the required element of $F$. ◻
]

The relevant covariance is exact. If $h$ carries $I$ affinely onto $I'$, then $h compose s_I = s_(I')$. Thus, if $g T^(\( n \))$ and $\( h g \) T^(\( n \))$ are basic and respect $I$ and $I'$, respectively,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.2)")
 $ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( h g \) T^(\( n \)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]_(I') = \( g T^(\( n \)) \)_I . $ <dyad:covariance>]

Here, as throughout, $h g = h compose g$. To verify the equality, match each source cell $C$ with its image $h \( C \)$ and apply $s_(I')^(- 1) compose h = s_I^(- 1)$ on $I$.

=== 2.2 The recursive coloring <sec:coloring>

Fix a real Hilbert space $H$, its closed unit ball $B$, and a Lipschitz map $f : B arrow.r B$ as in Lemma 2. Fix $L > 0$ and $delta > 0$ such that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.3)")
 $ lr(bar.v.double f \( x \) - f \( y \) bar.v.double) lt.eq L lr(bar.v.double x - y bar.v.double) \, #h(2em) lr(bar.v.double f \( x \) - x bar.v.double) gt.eq delta quad \( x \, y in B \) . $ <color:map>]

Choose an integer $D gt.eq 2$ so that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.4)")
 $ D > frac(4 L^2, delta^2) . $ <color:parameters>]

Choose internal basic intervals $I_1 < dots.h.c < I_D$. Such a family exists for every finite $D$: take $2^r > 2 D$ and use $I_j = \[ \( 2 j - 1 \) 2^(- r) \, 2 j 2^(- r) \]$.

Define a color $p \( T \) in B$ for every basic partition $T$ by induction on its number of cells:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.5)")
 $ p \( T \) = cases(delim: "{", f (1 / D sum_(j = 1)^D p \( T_(I_j) \)) \, & upright("if ") T upright(" respects every ") I_j \,, 0 \, & upright("otherwise") .) $ <color:recursion>]

This induction is well founded. In the first case each $T_(I_j)$ has strictly fewer cells than $T$, since $I_j$ is internal and $T$ has cells outside it. The average of the previously defined colors belongs to the convex ball $B$, so the argument of $f$ is always in its domain, and $p \( T \) in B$.

For the displayed choice $\| I_j \| = 2^(- r)$, the one-cell partition $T^(\( 0 \))$ has color zero, while

$ p \( T^(\( r \)) \) = f \( 0 \) \, #h(2em) p \( T^(\( 2 r \)) \) = f \( f \( 0 \) \) . $

Indeed, normalizing a restriction to any $I_j$ changes these uniform partition levels from $r$ to $0$ and from $2 r$ to $r$, respectively.

We use the finite family of parent intervals and their descendants

$ cal(I) = { I_i : 1 lt.eq i lt.eq D } union { I_i dot.op I_j : 1 lt.eq i \, j lt.eq D } . $

All these intervals are internal. Call $\( n \, g \)$ #emph[admissible] if $g T^(\( n \))$ is a basic partition respecting every member of $cal(I)$. For every $n$, every $g in F$, and every $I in cal(I)$, define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.6)")
 $ X_I^(\( n \)) \( g \) = cases(delim: "{", p #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( g T^(\( n \)) \)_I #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, & upright("if ") \( n \, g \) upright(" is admissible") \,, 0 \, & upright("otherwise") .) $ <color:global>]

In particular these are globally defined $B$-valued functions. Their scalar correlations

$ phi_(I \, J)^(\( n \)) \( g \) = chevron.l X_I^(\( n \)) \( g \) \, X_J^(\( n \)) \( g \) chevron.r $

are defined on all of $F$ and have absolute value at most one. By Lemma 3, every finite set of group elements is admissible at one common sufficiently large level $n$.

=== 2.3 A uniform boundary bound <sec:boundary>

For every ordered pair $I < J$ in $cal(I)$, choose, using Lemma 4, an element $h_(I \, J) in F$ carrying $I \, J$ affinely onto $I_1 \, I_2$, respectively. Let $S$ be the finite set of these elements. All choices so far, in particular $S$, are independent of the finite set to be tested.

For any nonempty finite $A subset F$, any $h in F$, and any $phi : F arrow.r \[ - 1 \, 1 \]$, cancellation over $h A ∩ A$ gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.7)")
 $ lr(|frac(1, \| A \|) sum_(g in A) phi \( h g \) - frac(1, \| A \|) sum_(g in A) phi \( g \)|) lt.eq frac(\| h A triangle.stroked.t A \|, \| A \|) . $ <eq:finite-translation>]

This is the finite-average comparison we will apply to the scalar correlations.

#thm([Proposition 2.3.])[
  #emph[For every nonempty finite set $A subset F$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.8)")
 $ max_(h in S) frac(\| h A triangle.stroked.t A \|, \| A \|) gt.eq frac(delta^2 \/ L^2 - 4 \/ D, 4 \( 1 - 1 \/ D \)) > 0 . $ <avg:boundary-bound>]

]
] <avg:boundary>

#proof[
  Fix such a set $A$ and put
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.9)")
   $ eta = max_(h in S) frac(\| h A triangle.stroked.t A \|, \| A \|) . $ <avg:defect>]
  Choose a single $n$ for which $\( n \, g \)$ is admissible for all
  $ g in A union S A \, #h(2em) S A = union.big_(h in S) h A . $
  This choice is possible because that union is finite; the level $n$ may depend on $A$. We now fix this $n$, omit its superscript, and write
  $ bb(E)_A psi = frac(1, \| A \|) sum_(g in A) psi \( g \) \, #h(2em) alpha = bb(E)_A phi_(I_1 \, I_2) . $
  ===== 2.3.1 Comparing correlations. <comparing-correlations.>
  For $I < J$ in $cal(I)$ and $g in A$, admissibility of both $g$ and $h_(I \, J) g$, together with (2.2), gives
  $ X_I \( g \) = X_(I_1) \( h_(I \, J) g \) \, #h(2em) X_J \( g \) = X_(I_2) \( h_(I \, J) g \) . $
  It follows that $phi_(I \, J) \( g \) = phi_(I_1 \, I_2) \( h_(I \, J) g \)$. Apply (2.7) to the globally bounded function $phi_(I_1 \, I_2)$ and use (2.9). We obtain
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.10)")
   $ lr(|bb(E)_A chevron.l X_I \, X_J chevron.r - alpha|) lt.eq eta #h(2em) upright("whenever ") I \, J in cal(I) upright(" are strictly separated") . $ <avg:correlation>]
  The reversed order follows from symmetry of the real inner product. No condition is imposed on nested pairs. Also, a transport $h_(I \, J)$ need not preserve the other intervals in $cal(I)$: admissibility was required separately at its source and target group elements.
  ===== 2.3.2 The variance estimate. <the-variance-estimate.>
  For $g in A$, put
  $ m \( g \) = 1 / D sum_(k = 1)^D X_(I_k) \( g \) \, #h(2em) z_i \( g \) = 1 / D sum_(j = 1)^D X_(I_i dot.op I_j) \( g \) quad \( 1 lt.eq i lt.eq D \) . $
  These vectors lie in $B$. Admissibility ensures that the partition $\( g T^(\( n \)) \)_(I_i)$ respects each $I_j$. Applying the recursion and (2.1) therefore yields the exact identities
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.11)")
   $ X_(I_i) \( g \) = f \( z_i \( g \) \) #h(2em) \( g in A \, 1 lt.eq i lt.eq D \) . $ <avg:recursive-identity>]
  Consequently, for every $g in A$, the displacement bound, the convexity of the squared norm, and the Lipschitz bound give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.12)")
   $ delta^2 & lt.eq lr(bar.v.double m \( g \) - f \( m \( g \) \) bar.v.double)^2\
 & = ∥1 / D sum_(i = 1)^D #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] f \( z_i \( g \) \) - f \( m \( g \) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]∥^2\
 & lt.eq 1 / D sum_(i = 1)^D lr(bar.v.double f \( z_i \( g \) \) - f \( m \( g \) \) bar.v.double)^2\
 & lt.eq L^2 / D sum_(i = 1)^D lr(bar.v.double z_i \( g \) - m \( g \) bar.v.double)^2 . $ <avg:displacement>]
  We bound the finite average of each squared distance in the last line. In the expansion of $lr(bar.v.double m bar.v.double)^2$, the $D \( D - 1 \)$ off-diagonal terms pair strictly separated parents, so (2.10) applies. Each of the $D$ diagonal terms is at most one. The same statements hold for the siblings in $lr(bar.v.double z_i bar.v.double)^2$. Hence
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.13)")
   $ bb(E)_A lr(bar.v.double m bar.v.double)^2 & lt.eq (1 - 1 / D) \( alpha + eta \) + 1 / D \,\
bb(E)_A lr(bar.v.double z_i bar.v.double)^2 & lt.eq (1 - 1 / D) \( alpha + eta \) + 1 / D . $ <avg:parent-bound>]
  In the mixed term
  $ chevron.l z_i \, m chevron.r = 1 / D^2 sum_(j = 1)^D sum_(k = 1)^D chevron.l X_(I_i dot.op I_j) \, X_(I_k) chevron.r \, $
  the $D \( D - 1 \)$ terms with $k eq.not i$ pair strictly separated intervals. Each has average at least $alpha - eta$. The remaining $D$ terms pair a descendant with its own parent; each is at least $- 1$ by the unit-ball bound. Figure 1 shows this division of the mixed pairs. Thus
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.14)")
   $ bb(E)_A chevron.l z_i \, m chevron.r gt.eq (1 - 1 / D) \( alpha - eta \) - 1 / D . $ <avg:mixed-bound>]
