#proof[
  The fixed finite set $S$ and the uniform positive lower bound in Proposition 5 violate the Følner criterion (1.1). Hence $F$ is not amenable. ◻
]

== 3 Consequences <sec:consequences>

Theorem 1 allows us to apply two companion results for nonamenable groups. The first concerns representations that are uniformly close to being isometric but cannot be made unitary by an equivalent Hilbert norm. The second concerns percolation on the simple Cayley graphs of $F$ associated with finite symmetric generating sets. Neither companion result is used in the proof of nonamenability.

=== 3.1 Uniformly bounded representations <uniformly-bounded-representations>

#thm([Corollary 3.1.])[
  #emph[For every $epsilon > 0$, there are a separable complex Hilbert space $H$ and a representation $pi : F arrow.r upright("GL") \( H \)$ such that $sup_(g in F) lr(bar.v.double pi \( g \) bar.v.double) lt.eq 1 + epsilon$, but no bounded invertible operator $S$ on $H$ makes every $S pi \( g \) S^(- 1)$ unitary.]
] <cor:nonunitarizable>

#proof[
  The group $F$ is countable, by its finite dyadic piecewise linear description, and nonamenable by Theorem 1, so the conclusion follows from the companion unitarizability theorem #cite(label("OpenAIUnitarizability2026")) Theorem 1.1. ◻
]

=== 3.2 Percolation on Cayley graphs <percolation-on-cayley-graphs>

For a finite symmetric generating set $S subset F \\ { upright("id") }$, let $G_S$ be the simple undirected Cayley graph with edges ${ x \, x s }$. In Bernoulli bond percolation on this fixed graph, each edge is independently open with probability $p$. Write $p_c$ for the threshold for existence of an infinite open cluster and $p_u$ for the threshold for almost-sure uniqueness of the infinite open cluster. The connection kernel $T_p \( x \, y \) = bb(P)_p \( x arrow.l.r y \)$ acts on counting-measure $ell^2 \( F \)$, initially on finitely supported functions by summation over $y$; put $p_(2 arrow.r 2) = sup { p in \[ 0 \, 1 \] : T_p upright(" is bounded on ") ell^2 \( F \) }$.

#thm([Corollary 3.2.])[
  #emph[For every such generating set $S$, 
$ lr(bar.v.double T_(p_c) bar.v.double)_(2 arrow.r 2) < oo \, #h(2em) p_c < p_(2 arrow.r 2) lt.eq p_u . $

 For each fixed $p in \( p_c \, p_u \)$, there are almost surely infinitely many infinite open clusters. Moreover, there are deterministic $p_c < p_1 < p_2 < 1$, depending on $S$, such that in the coupling with independent uniform $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$ edge labels $U_e$, opening $e$ when $U_e lt.eq p$, there are almost surely infinitely many infinite open clusters simultaneously for every $p in \[ p_1 \, p_2 \]$.]
] <cor:percolation>

#proof[
  Apply the companion percolation result #cite(label("OpenAIPercolation2026")) Corollary 1.2 to $F$, which is nonamenable by Theorem 1. ◻
]

== 4 An explicit map with positive displacement <app:auxiliary>

We give a direct construction of the map needed in Lemma 2. This proves the Hilbert-space instance of the Benyamini–Sternfeld theorem #cite(label("BenyaminiSternfeld1983")) directly. The aim is to construct a Lipschitz map $G : B arrow.r H$ bounded away from zero and equal to the identity when $lr(bar.v.double x bar.v.double) gt.eq 1 \/ 2$. Then $f \( x \) = - G \( x \) \/ lr(bar.v.double G \( x \) bar.v.double)$ has the required displacement. A tube about a curve with a bounded tail supplies $G$.

#thm([Proposition 4.1.])[
  #emph[Let $H = L^2 \( \[ 0 \, 1 \] \; bb(R)^2 \)$ be a real Hilbert space and let $B = { x in H : lr(bar.v.double x bar.v.double) lt.eq 1 }$. There is a globally Lipschitz map $f : B arrow.r B$ such that 
$ lr(bar.v.double f \( x \) bar.v.double) = 1 \, #h(2em) lr(bar.v.double f \( x \) - x bar.v.double) gt.eq 1 / 2 quad \( x in B \) . $

]
] <aux:map>

Define functions on $bb(R)$ by

$ a \( t \) = cases(delim: "{", t \/ 8 \, & t lt.eq 2 \,, 5 \/ 16 - \( 3 - t \)^2 \/ 16 \, & 2 < t < 3 \,, 5 \/ 16 \, & t gt.eq 3 \,) #h(2em) theta \( t \) = cases(delim: "{", 0 \, & t lt.eq 1 \,, \( t - 1 \)^2 \/ 2 \, & 1 < t < 2 \,, t - 3 \/ 2 \, & t gt.eq 2 .) $

For $w in \[ 0 \, 1 \]$, put

$ v \( t \) \( w \) = \( cos \( theta \( t \) w \) \, sin \( theta \( t \) w \) \) \, #h(2em) gamma \( t \) = a \( t \) v \( t \) . $

Thus $lr(bar.v.double v \( t \) bar.v.double) = 1$, $gamma \( 0 \) = 0$, and $gamma$ follows a straight line for $t lt.eq 1$; its tail for $t gt.eq 1$ lies in the ball of radius $5 \/ 16$. The next lemma shows that, although the positive tail is bounded, curve points whose parameters differ by a fixed amount remain uniformly separated. This permits a tube of fixed radius along the whole curve.

#thm([Lemma 4.2.])[
  #emph[The curve $gamma$ is injective, has a bounded Lipschitz derivative, and 
$ c := inf_(t in bb(R)) lr(bar.v.double gamma' \( t \) bar.v.double) = 1 / 8 . $

 Its unit tangent $u \( t \) = gamma' \( t \) \/ lr(bar.v.double gamma' \( t \) bar.v.double)$ is Lipschitz, $chevron.l u \( t \) \, v \( t \) chevron.r gt.eq 0$, and $u \( t \) = v \( t \)$ for $t lt.eq 1$. There is a radius $0 < rho < 1 \/ 32$ such that every point in 
$ U = { x in H : "dist" \( x \, gamma \( bb(R) \) \) < rho } $

 has a unique nearest point $gamma \( t \( x \) \)$. Writing $e \( x \) = x - gamma \( t \( x \) \)$, we have $e \( x \) perp u \( t \( x \) \)$, and 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.1)")
 $ \| t \( x \) - t \( y \) \| lt.eq 16 lr(bar.v.double x - y bar.v.double) quad upright("if ") x \, y in U upright(" and ") lr(bar.v.double x - y bar.v.double) < rho . $ <aux:parameter>]

]
] <aux:tube>

#proof[
  Let $J \( xi_1 \, xi_2 \) = \( - xi_2 \, xi_1 \)$. Since $0 lt.eq w lt.eq 1$, the scalar trigonometric Taylor remainders are uniform in $w$, which justifies differentiation in $H$. We obtain
  $ gamma' \( t \) \( w \) = a' \( t \) v \( t \) \( w \) + a \( t \) theta' \( t \) w J v \( t \) \( w \) \, #h(2em) lr(bar.v.double gamma' \( t \) bar.v.double)^2 = a' \( t \)^2 + frac(a \( t \)^2 theta' \( t \)^2, 3) . $
  For $t lt.eq 2$ the first term is $1 \/ 64$; for $t gt.eq 2$ we have $a \( t \) gt.eq 1 \/ 4$ and $theta' \( t \) = 1$. Equality in the claimed lower speed bound holds for $t lt.eq 1$. The first derivatives match at the junctions $1 \, 2 \, 3$, and their derivatives on the intervening intervals are bounded. On the unbounded negative interval the angular derivatives vanish. Consequently $gamma'$ is bounded and Lipschitz. The same is true of $v$ and $u$, and $chevron.l u \( t \) \, v \( t \) chevron.r = a' \( t \) \/ lr(bar.v.double gamma' \( t \) bar.v.double) gt.eq 0$.
  We need the following uniform separation property:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.2)")
   $ sigma \( d \) := inf_(\| t - s \| gt.eq d) lr(bar.v.double gamma \( t \) - gamma \( s \) bar.v.double) > 0 #h(2em) \( d > 0 \) . $ <aux:separation>]
  Indeed, with $"sinc" \( q \) = sin \( q \) \/ q$ for $q eq.not 0$ and $"sinc" \( 0 \) = 1$, we have
  $ chevron.l v \( s \) \, v \( t \) chevron.r = "sinc" \( theta \( t \) - theta \( s \) \) . $
  Since $\| "sinc" \( q \) \| < 1$ for $q eq.not 0$, distinct angular parameters give noncollinear unit vectors. On $\( - oo \, 1 \]$ the coefficient $a$ is strictly increasing, whereas $theta$ is strictly increasing on $\( 1 \, oo \)$ and $a$ is positive there. This proves injectivity.
  If (4.2) failed, choose $s_n < t_n$ with $t_n - s_n gt.eq d$ and $lr(bar.v.double gamma \( t_n \) - gamma \( s_n \) bar.v.double) arrow.r 0$, and pass to subsequences for which both parameters have limits in the extended real line. Finite limits contradict injectivity. If both limits are $- oo$, the distances equal $\( t_n - s_n \) \/ 8$ eventually. If only $s_n arrow.r - oo$, the first radii tend to infinity while the second remain bounded. If $s_n arrow.r s in bb(R)$ and $t_n arrow.r + oo$, the displayed inner product tends to zero, and the squared distances tend to $a \( s \)^2 + \( 5 \/ 16 \)^2$. Finally, if both parameters tend to $+ oo$, then eventually
  $ lr(bar.v.double gamma \( t_n \) - gamma \( s_n \) bar.v.double)^2 = 2 \( 5 \/ 16 \)^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 - "sinc" \( t_n - s_n \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  This is bounded away from zero because $sup_(q gt.eq d) "sinc" \( q \) < 1$, by continuity and decay at infinity. These cases establish (4.2).
  Write $K = "Lip" \( gamma' \)$ and $K_u = "Lip" \( u \)$. Choose $d > 0$ with $K d lt.eq c \/ 2$. The estimate
  $ lr(bar.v.double gamma \( t \) - gamma \( s \) - \( t - s \) gamma' \( s \) bar.v.double) lt.eq K / 2 \| t - s \|^2 $
  then implies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.3)")
   $ \| chevron.l gamma \( t \) - gamma \( s \) \, u \( s \) chevron.r \| gt.eq frac(3 c, 4) \| t - s \| #h(2em) \( \| t - s \| lt.eq d \) . $ <aux:tangent>]
  Choose $rho > 0$ so that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.4)")
   $ rho < 1 / 32 \, #h(2em) 4 rho < sigma \( d \) \, #h(2em) rho K_u < c / 4 . $ <aux:radius>]
  For $x in U$, take a minimizing sequence $gamma \( t_n \)$ for the distance from $x$ to the curve. Its late terms satisfy $lr(bar.v.double x - gamma \( t_n \) bar.v.double) < 2 rho$, so their pairwise distances are less than $4 rho$. By (4.2) their parameters differ by less than $d$. Fixing one late parameter therefore confines all later ones to a compact real interval. A convergent subsequence attains the minimum. Differentiating $lr(bar.v.double x - gamma \( t \) bar.v.double)^2$ at any minimizing parameter gives $\( x - gamma \( t \) \) perp u \( t \)$.
  For $x \, y in U$ with $lr(bar.v.double x - y bar.v.double) < rho$, choose any minimizing parameters $t \, s$ and write $x = gamma \( t \) + e_t$, $y = gamma \( s \) + e_s$. The curve points are less than $3 rho$ apart, so $\| t - s \| < d$. Orthogonality and (4.3) give
  $ lr(bar.v.double x - y bar.v.double) & gt.eq \| chevron.l gamma \( t \) - gamma \( s \) \, u \( s \) chevron.r \| - \| chevron.l e_t \, u \( s \) - u \( t \) chevron.r \|\
 & gt.eq (frac(3 c, 4) - rho K_u) \| t - s \| gt.eq c / 2 \| t - s \| . $
  Taking $y = x$ proves uniqueness; the same inequality proves (4.1). ◻
]

We next arrange that a residual perpendicular to the curve’s tangent is carried to a vector perpendicular to $v \( t \)$. This will let us change the coefficient along $v \( t \)$ without cancellation by that residual.

#thm([Lemma 4.3.])[
  #emph[There is a family of orthogonal operators $R_t : H arrow.r H$, Lipschitz in operator norm, such that $R_t u \( t \) = v \( t \)$ and $R_t$ is the identity for $t lt.eq 1$.]
] <aux:rotation>

#proof[
  Suppress $t$ and put $kappa = chevron.l u \, v chevron.r gt.eq 0$. Define
  $ A h = v chevron.l u \, h chevron.r - u chevron.l v \, h chevron.r \, #h(2em) R = upright("Id") + A + frac(A^2, 1 + kappa) . $
  If $u \, v$ are independent, then $A$ is skew-adjoint, $A^2 = - \( 1 - kappa^2 \) upright("Id")$ on their span, and $R$ restricts there to $kappa upright("Id") + A$. This restriction is orthogonal and sends $u$ to $v$, while $R$ is the identity on the orthogonal complement. If $u = v$, then $A = 0$ and $R = upright("Id")$. These exhaust the cases because $kappa gt.eq 0$. The formula, the Lipschitz dependence of $u \, v$, and $1 + kappa gt.eq 1$ show that $t mapsto R_t$ is Lipschitz in operator norm, including where $u = v$. ◻
]

#proof[
  Fix the tube radius from Lemma 9 and the rotations from Lemma 10. Set
  $ b \( t \) = min { a \( t \) \, - 1 \/ 8 } \, #h(2em) q \( t \) = b \( t \) - a \( t \) = - max { a \( t \) + 1 \/ 8 \, 0 } . $
  Then $\| q \( t \) \| lt.eq 7 \/ 16$ and $"Lip" \( q \) lt.eq 1 \/ 8$. Let $beta \, chi : \[ 0 \, oo \) arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ be the continuous piecewise linear functions specified by
  $ beta \( r \) = cases(delim: "{", 1 \, & r lt.eq rho \/ 4 \,, 2 - 4 r \/ rho \, & rho \/ 4 < r < rho \/ 2 \,, 0 \, & r gt.eq rho \/ 2 \,) #h(2em) chi \( r \) = cases(delim: "{", 1 \, & r lt.eq rho \/ 2 \,, 2 - 2 r \/ rho \, & rho \/ 2 < r < rho \,, 0 \, & r gt.eq rho .) $
  Define $G : B arrow.r H$ to equal the identity outside $U$. For $x in B ∩ U$, write $t = t \( x \)$, $e = x - gamma \( t \)$, $r = lr(bar.v.double e bar.v.double)$, and set
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.5)")
   $ G \( x \) & = gamma \( t \) + beta \( r \) q \( t \) v \( t \) + #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( 1 - chi \( r \) \) upright("Id") + chi \( r \) R_t #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] e\
 & = x + beta \( r \) q \( t \) v \( t \) + chi \( r \) \( R_t - upright("Id") \) e . $ <aux:G>]
  First, $G$ is globally Lipschitz on $B$. For tube points at distance less than $rho$, (4.1) controls their parameters, and, with $M = sup_t lr(bar.v.double gamma' \( t \) bar.v.double)$, their residuals satisfy
  $ lr(bar.v.double e \( x \) - e \( y \) bar.v.double) lt.eq \( 1 + 16 M \) lr(bar.v.double x - y bar.v.double) . $
  Moreover, $r \( x \) = "dist" \( x \, gamma \( bb(R) \) \)$ is $1$-Lipschitz. Every factor in the second line of (4.5) is therefore Lipschitz for such pairs with uniform constants; the factors are uniformly bounded, using $lr(bar.v.double e bar.v.double) < rho$, $\| q \| lt.eq 7 \/ 16$, $lr(bar.v.double v bar.v.double) = 1$ and $lr(bar.v.double R_t bar.v.double) = 1$. The product estimates give one finite Lipschitz bound for all these pairs. Also, on all of $B$,
  $ lr(bar.v.double G \( x \) bar.v.double) lt.eq 1 + 7 \/ 16 + 2 rho < 3 \/ 2 \, $
  so pairs at distance at least $rho$ are controlled by $3 \/ rho$. For the remaining pairs, let $x in U$ and $y in.not U$. Both cutoffs satisfy $beta \( r \) \, chi \( r \) lt.eq 2 \( rho - r \) \/ rho$, whence
  $ lr(bar.v.double G \( x \) - x bar.v.double) lt.eq (frac(7, 8 rho) + 4) \( rho - r \) . $
  Since distance to the curve is $1$-Lipschitz and $y in.not U$, $rho - r lt.eq lr(bar.v.double x - y bar.v.double)$. Together with $G \( y \) = y$, this proves the required bound across the tube boundary. Outside the tube $G$ is the identity.
  Next, we claim that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.6)")
   $ lr(bar.v.double G \( x \) bar.v.double) gt.eq rho \/ 4 #h(2em) \( x in B \) . $ <aux:nonzero>]
  Outside $U$, the fact that $0 = gamma \( 0 \)$ gives $lr(bar.v.double G \( x \) bar.v.double) = lr(bar.v.double x bar.v.double) gt.eq rho$. Inside $U$, if $r lt.eq rho \/ 2$, then $chi \( r \) = 1$ and $R_t e perp v \( t \)$, with $lr(bar.v.double R_t e bar.v.double) = r$. For $r lt.eq rho \/ 4$, the coefficient along $v \( t \)$ is $b \( t \) lt.eq - 1 \/ 8$; for $rho \/ 4 lt.eq r lt.eq rho \/ 2$, the perpendicular component has norm at least $rho \/ 4$. If $rho \/ 2 lt.eq r < rho$, then $beta \( r \) = 0$ and the last term in the first line of (4.5) has norm at most $r < rho$. When $t > 1$, we have $a \( t \) gt.eq 1 \/ 8$, so $lr(bar.v.double G \( x \) bar.v.double) gt.eq 1 \/ 8 - rho > rho \/ 4$. When $t lt.eq 1$, we have $R_t = upright("Id")$ and $e perp v \( t \)$, so $lr(bar.v.double G \( x \) bar.v.double) gt.eq r gt.eq rho \/ 2$. This proves (4.6).
  Finally, $G \( x \) = x$ whenever $lr(bar.v.double x bar.v.double) gt.eq 1 \/ 2$. This holds by definition outside $U$. Inside $U$, the reverse triangle inequality gives
  $ \| a \( t \) \| = lr(bar.v.double gamma \( t \) bar.v.double) gt.eq lr(bar.v.double x bar.v.double) - r > 1 \/ 2 - rho > 5 \/ 16 . $
  Since $a \( t \) lt.eq 5 \/ 16$ everywhere, this forces $a \( t \) < - 1 \/ 8$ and $t lt.eq 1$. Thus $q \( t \) = 0$ and $R_t = upright("Id")$, so (4.5) again gives $G \( x \) = x$.
  Define
  $ f \( x \) = - frac(G \( x \), lr(bar.v.double G \( x \) bar.v.double)) . $
  Normalization on vectors of norm at least $rho \/ 4$ is Lipschitz with constant at most $8 \/ rho$, so $f$ is globally Lipschitz by (4.6), and its values have norm one. If $lr(bar.v.double x bar.v.double) < 1 \/ 2$, then $lr(bar.v.double f \( x \) - x bar.v.double) gt.eq 1 - lr(bar.v.double x bar.v.double) > 1 \/ 2$. Otherwise $G \( x \) = x$, and $lr(bar.v.double f \( x \) - x bar.v.double) = 1 + lr(bar.v.double x bar.v.double) gt.eq 3 \/ 2$. ◻
]


